{
	"Name": "OKTA_TUNING",
	"Description": "Noise control for Okta detections. Suppresses events involving accounts and source addresses you have vouched for, and force-promotes events involving accounts and addresses you are watching. Expand the macro in the search GUI to read every rule inline; nothing is hidden behind further macros.\n\nRequires these enumerated values to exist before the macro runs:\n  - actorUserNorm, targetUserNorm: lower-cased actor and target usernames with the domain stripped\n  - clientIp, requestIp: from client.ipAddress and request.ipChain.[0].ip\n  - published: the Okta event timestamp\n  - risk_score, _priority: from $OKTA_RISK_SCORE\n\nResources, all keyed on a bare value (an IP address, or a lower-cased username with the domain stripped):\n  - okta_user_allowlist: key,enabled,until_ymd,reason\n  - okta_ip_allowlist: key,enabled,until_ymd,reason\n  - okta_user_denylist: key,enabled,min_risk,reason\n  - okta_ip_denylist: key,enabled,min_risk,reason\n\nAllowlist rows drop the event outright. Set enabled=1 and leave until_ymd empty for a permanent exception, or set it to a YYYYMMDD date for one that expires. Denylist rows raise risk_score to at least 75 (priority High) once risk_score reaches min_risk; leave min_risk empty or 0 to always promote.\n\nProduces:\n  - src: the source address, clientIp preferred, else requestIp\n  - _force_alert: 1 when a denylist row promoted this event, else 0\n  - risk_score and _priority, possibly raised\n\nBehaviour, in six commented steps:\n  1. Resolve src from clientIp, falling back to requestIp.\n  2. Build the actor and target lookup keys, using \"-\" when an account is absent.\n  3. Convert published into a YYYYMMDD integer for expiry comparison.\n  4. Look the actor, target, and source up in the allowlists; drop the event on any enabled, unexpired hit.\n  5. Look the same three values up in the denylists and set _force_alert.\n  6. Promote force-alerted events to at least risk_score 75 / priority High.\n\nUsage:\n$OKTA_RISK_SCORE\n$OKTA_TUNING\n\nPlacement: always after $OKTA_RISK_SCORE, because the denylist min_risk comparison reads risk_score.",
	"Labels": [
		"okta"
	]
}